hey everybody welcome to the box this is  a little one off planet coaster project  that I decided to work on just because I  had the idea and I just couldn't help  but turn it into something real to be  honest though I've had this idea for a  really long time for a few years now  even and to be completely honest it's  not even my idea  in 2008 a YouTube user by the name of  moon Kim for the win uploaded a  rollercoaster tycoon 3 coaster called  the box and this turned out to be one of  the most popular rollercoaster tycoon 3  videos ever and because it was such an  easy to do formats because really all he  did was create a simple rollercoaster  layout and then build a check a sort of  checkered pattern building around it to  make it feel like a box so because of  this very simple strategy to making this  video it's one that God repeated many  many times over by different people and  at this point I think there are over 50  the box videos on YouTube and that is  really just the standard the box series  made by different people there are all  kinds of variations on this as well with  all kinds of different color schemes  there's also a video called up is down  by Luke Lord which I believe is probably  inspired by the books because it's  pretty much just a the box coaster with  a different building around it but the  same format exists for all of these  videos it is always a coaster inside a  sort of chessboard style building where  you basically kind of lose your  orientation sometimes regarding what is  up and what is down and somebody has  made a box coaster in planet coaster as  well but personally I've always wanted  to try and take this idea and take it to  the next level in a sense that I wanted  to try it in a way that would be  realistically plausible a lot of the box  coasters are huge completely unrealistic  and would never be built in real life  and most definitely don't tend to have  layouts that would  work in real life featuring all kinds of  inversions and things like that are  completely impossible so I would love to  try and see if it's possible to make a  quote-unquote realistic the box coaster  that at least you know could be feasibly  built and survivable if you ride it and  in addition to that I've always had some  lighting issues with the box coasters  because usually the lighting was kind of  unrealistic so for this one I'm going to  try to make it dark and actually light  it up with lights as well and finally  just as a way to add some flare to the  whole disorienting experience of it I  decided to make it a mouse on a spinning  coaster specifically the one that can go  upside down which the game technically  doesn't have also what am I saying Mario  Sona it's a Mac spinning coaster but  yeah it's something that works it's  based on I guess time traveler in real  life although there are now going to be  more of these inverting spinning  coasters popping up in different  countries as well I believe pop salons  Belgium is going to get one as well  either way they're a hot new thing on  the block and they're probably one of  the coolest most disorienting coasters  out there so to me it felt like the  perfect opportunity to make a the book  style coaster that is truly disorienting  and hopefully to some extent realistic  as well so as you can see I've worked  out the basic layout for the coaster  honestly I didn't put too much time into  actually making it smooth because it's  it would take a lot of time with not  that much return-on-investment I figured  especially given the fact that it's a  spinning coaster I don't think that  little pumps in the layout will be that  noticeable but the layout itself is also  just quite simple it mostly features a  lot of dive loops and over-banked turns  because these are the elements that well  for one they fit very well into this  super limited amount of space that I  have inside this box but also I think  they're really disorienting elements  they're elements where you can just  constantly Bank in different directions  and make it very confusing whether  you're upside down or not just make it  confusing in general whether you're  going  through a half loop or just going  through a curve on one of the sides of  this box it's just I feel like these  elements flow really well into each  other when it comes to trying to make a  disorienting coaster where you can't  tell what's up and down but at the same  time not a coaster where the g-forces  are gonna be ridiculous  so at any time especially during the  inversions you will have positive forces  on this coaster there is no air time on  it of course there's some hang time but  you never get unrealistic moments where  you're hanging by your lap bar on a  curve that is banked the other way  around or anything like that's based on  g-forces this coaster should be pretty  writable and not subject the people - to  crazy forces so really my only concern  at this point is that if you were to  write this in real life you might be  able to tell from the g-forces whether  you're up or down but I also have to say  that at the end of the day positive  g-forces are positive g-forces so I  don't think that given this sort of  environment your body would be very good  at telling whether you're going through  positive g-forces in a turn or whether  you're going through positive g-force in  a loop but that's hard to tell because  this kind of coaster doesn't really  exist in real life  now one of the other tricks that I try  to use to feasibly make this coaster  feel much more confusing is I tried to  connect supports to the closest wall to  whichever track piece I was supporting  so down on the ground here you can see  that the supports are just simply  vertical as they would normally be but  then on the sides when the track comes  close to one of the walls of the box I  decided to add supports that are  horizontal so it kind of feels like  those walls could be you know the ground  as well and then when it comes to the  track pieces that are on the ceiling  those are attached also to the ceiling  it also kind of helps to not make this  entire box a giant mess of supports  because with this layout it would  definitely turn into that if I were to  connect everything to the ground now I'm  not entirely sure you know on an  engineering level how feasible this is  but in my mind  and you just have supports that are  suspended from the ceiling on top and  then the supports on the side even  though they connect to the walls in a  strange way I did add you know diagonal  lateral supports to it as well so from  my knowledge I think this thing should  be able to hold its weight obviously I'm  not an engineer who like calculates  exactly what the forces on the track are  going to be and whether it would work  out well but I think it's plausible  enough that a co so we could be built  this way so if you look for instance at  the support that I just built it's one  support that's on the side of the walls  but you can see that there's a  horizontal beam and then there is a  diagonal beam underneath that supporting  net  so that's realistic enough but at the  same time it looks like a support that  could also be attached to the ground you  never know  now that is everything I did for the  main chaotic piece of this layouts but  there is a mid-course break run where  you're sort of disorienting illusion  gets broken for a second just because  for well I guess realism reasons I also  wanted to give this write a decent  throughput and these trains aren't too  large so I feel like the capacity of  this ride would be way too limited if it  didn't have a mid-course brake run so  after all of the madness inside that box  we go into a mid-course brake run where  for a second I think you get a decent  idea of what is up and down and also  after that I didn't have too much height  to play with anymore so I couldn't do  any really crazy elements anymore but  there was one thing that I've always  wanted to try and that is build a  corkscrew with a spinning coaster inside  a room where you also can tell what is  up or down so that is really the the  focus of this set at the second section  of the ride right here other than that I  have to say that I'm just going to theme  everything in a super basic way there  are just going to be concrete walls all  around this thing because it's not  really supposed to have a theme aside  from being the box  I guess it's definitely not here to be a  beautiful ride really my only goal is to  disorient people with layouts  would honestly just be feasible and  understandable outside of the context of  this disorienting box so for this  corkscrew I'm just building a small mini  box with the same checkerboard pattern  and I'm not sure you know if it looks  very disorienting right now but honestly  when I tried testing this thing even  knowing what this row is like it's  really hard to tell what's up and down  as you're on the right because with a  spinning coaster you start losing points  of reference and I think this is a  crucial difference with a regular  coaster I think you can always find  these little points of reference where  you can tell whether you're upside down  or not depending on you know in real  life how you feel but even in the game  you can see it you can see sometimes  where the ground is or you can have a  feeling for what elements are shaped  like and you can recognize which part of  an element would be you know upside down  and which wouldn't so for a loop for  instance you can always see that the the  tightest part of the inversion is always  the top and it's always this at the  section where upside down because that's  where you have the slowest speeds but  you still maintain some of the same  g-force  so of course loops are always teardrop  shaped so just based on that track  shaping you can usually tell whether  you're upside down or not but with the  spinning coaster I think it's different  because you just start spinning in all  these crazy directions especially if the  spin is completely unlocked so there's  not a lot to hold on to you just  constantly see different things and even  inside this roll which is really just  one simple corkscrew and really  shouldn't be all that disorienting  anyway as soon as you start spinning  into that thing and you're spinning  because well the track is literally  rotating but at the same time the car is  also spinning on its own axis it just  gets too confusing to keep track of  where you're going I feel like you could  compare this maybe two okay so for my  experience perhaps the vertigo flat ride  in Tivoli Gardens in Copenhagen which  honestly is my favorite flat ride in the  world but it has two major sections a  has one part of the right where it spins  just really quickly it's just a plane on  a stick that is literally just what the  whole flat right is it's a plane on a  giant stick like a one plane Ferris  wheel that just spins really fast it  sounds boring but it's so intense that  it's honestly a really cool flat ride  but it also has a section of the ride  where it's not just the the large metal  arm that the plane is attached to that  spins but also the plane starts spinning  on its own axis and I think it creates a  similar effect to what you get with an  inverting spinning coaster I feel like  your body can maintain a decent idea of  what direction you're going if you're  spinning in one direction but as soon as  two different directions of spinning in  a 3d space are involved it just becomes  way too confusing so I'm kind of relying  also on that in this whole build now I  have to say at this point the coaster  itself is pretty much finished I just  wanted to finish off this whole thing by  adding a sort of exterior area around it  but it's just kind of I don't know how I  should put it it's just kind of for my  own sense of suspense of disbelief then  it could be a real coaster because at  the end of the day I'm not actually  gonna theme the outside of this or  anything it's just there to make it  believable that it could be a real-life  coaster inside a real knife dark ride  box which to be fair I think the  building could also reasonably fit into  because it's not that large of a  building and as you might have noticed I  did sink the layout into the ground so  that in the building wouldn't stick out  above the landscape that much so yeah  all things considered it's not you know  bigger than comparable indoor coasters  like rock on roller coaster for instance  so all things considered I I would  consider this as sort of concepts of  what the theme parks could do I don't  think anything Park is ever going to  build an indoor coaster like this but if  anybody ever comes up with the idea and  has the money for it then here's the  concept I think it's something cool and  something feasible honestly  that would be really cool to see in real  life we know we have the technology for  inverting spinning coasters now let's  make one indoors and make it so that you  can never tell which way you're going  that would be super awesome anyway  that's it for the time-lapse I'm gonna  leave you guys with some cinematic  footage and a POV of course and I hope  you enjoyed this little experiment bye  everybody  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]
 

